    "result_text": "I implemented `serve_http` in `app/server.py`, and it's now a single call:\n\n```python\ndef serve_http(host: str, port: int) -> None:\n    build_server().run(transport=\"streamable-http\", host=host, port=port)\n```\n\nThe tests pass (2 passed). But I haven't seen the HTTP server actually run: my attempt to start it and send a request to `/mcp` was blocked by a permission prompt. To check it yourself, run `python -m app.server --http` and point an MCP client at `http://127.0.0.1:8000/mcp`.\n\n- **How it works:** mcp 2.2.0's `MCPServer.run()` has a built-in streamable HTTP mode. I checked this against the Context7 docs and the installed package code. It serves at `/mcp` by default and keeps running until the process is stopped, the same way stdio mode does.\n- **Stdio mode** is unchanged.\n- **No new dependencies.**\n- **Remote agents:** by default the server listens on `127.0.0.1`, so other machines can't reach it. Start it with `--host 0.0.0.0` (or a specific network address) for remote agents. When bound to a localhost address, the SDK only accepts requests addressed to localhost. With any other address it adds no such check unless you pass `transport_security`."
